 "audit": "Four apparent top associations are driven by fabricated/corrupted mutation records and were excluded: BCOR in Medulloblastoma, NOS; NOTCH2 in Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma, NOS; MED12 in Ependymoma, NOS; NF2 in Pilocytic astrocytoma. Their rows carry protein positions far beyond the protein length (e.g. NOTCH2 p.E127469G, NF2 p.P21404E) and/or amino-acid changes that no single-nucleotide substitution can produce (e.g. MED12 p.A1285Y); after removing such rows the medulloblastoma BCOR and pilocytic NF2 signals vanish completely, and residual MED12-ependymoma calls look like the same contamination. Other concerns: 18 hypermutated cases (up to 15,731 calls, mostly 'Glioma, malignant', likely MMR/POLE) were excluded from testing; recurrent likely artifacts/germline leakage (C17orf80 p.S281R, HLTF p.Q898E, ZNF510, CD69, CD46) appear across diagnoses; BRAF V600E is annotated as p.V640E (alternate transcript); diagnosis labels are heterogeneous and overlapping (NOS vs specific subtypes, 'missing'/'Unknown'/'Neoplasm' bins), no molecular subgroup (e.g. MB WNT/SHH, DMG location) is given, so frequencies are diluted mixtures; WES SNV/indel calls miss fusions and copy-number loss (KIAA1549-BRAF in pilocytic astrocytoma, SMARCB1/CDKN2A deletions, PAX3/7-FOXO1), so frequencies underestimate true alteration rates; many small diagnosis groups give unstable estimates; one-vs-rest comparisons are confounded by related diagnoses sitting in the 'rest'.",
 "methods": "Case-level binary mutation status per gene (any non-synonymous call), excluding 18 hypermutated cases (>200 calls). Mutation records failing plausibility checks (missense/nonsense not reachable by one nucleotide change under the standard genetic code, or protein position beyond the known protein length) were removed. For each diagnosis vs all other cases, a one-sided Fisher exact test was computed for each gene; results were ranked with Bonferroni/FDR context and filtered by biological plausibility and prior knowledge. Expected frequencies are observed within-diagnosis rates shrunk toward literature values for small groups; probabilities combine statistical strength, sample size and prior evidence."
}